          "text": "# Plan: AI-Generated Go HTTP Server Project\n\nSince I'm in Plan mode, I'll lay out the full plan here in conversation rather than writing files. You can hand this off to an implementation agent later.\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure Overview\n\n**Goal:** Students start with AI-generated baseline code, then layer in class-based networking knowledge and one focused security track.\n\n**Suggested phases:**\n1. **AI-generated baseline** — minimal working Go HTTP server\n2. **Class-knowledge improvements** — students harden it with networking concepts\n3. **Security track** — one deep security theme (see below)\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: What the AI-Generated Baseline Should Look Like\n\nThe initial code should be simple enough that students understand every line, but complete enough to run. Suggested scope:\n\n| Feature | Details |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Language/stdlib** | Go, standard library `net/http` only (no frameworks) |\n| **Routes** | `GET /`, `GET /hello`, `GET /echo`, `POST /submit` |\n| **Status codes** | `200 OK`, `404 Not Found`, `405 Method Not Allowed`, `500 Internal Server Error` |\n| **Headers** | `Content-Type` (text/plain, text/html, application/json) |\n| **Body handling** | Read request body on POST, write response body |\n| **Logging** | Simple `log.Printf` for each request (method, path, status) |\n| **Server config** | `http.ListenAndServe(\":8080\", nil)` or a custom mux |\n| **Graceful shutdown** | Optional `http.Server` with `Shutdown` context |\n\n**Deliverable:** A single `main.go` (maybe split into `handler.go` + `main.go`) plus a `README.md` explaining how to run and test with `curl`.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: In-Class Knowledge Improvements (Student Work)\n\nThis is where students apply networking coursework. Suggested additions:\n\n- **Concurrency:** Explain how Go's `net/http` handles each request in a goroutine; add a request counter with `sync/atomic` or `sync.Mutex` to demonstrate thread safety.\n- **Connection management:** Set `ReadHeaderTimeout`, `WriteTimeout`, `IdleTimeout` on `http.Server` to prevent slowloris-style connection exhaustion.\n- **HTTP/1.1 semantics:** Proper `Connection: keep-alive` vs `close`, `Content-Length` vs chunked encoding, `Host` header handling.\n- **Status-code correctness:** Return `415 Unsupported Media Type` for wrong `Content-Type`, `400 Bad Request` for malformed bodies.\n- **Basic routing without a framework:** Use `http.ServeMux` and show pattern matching (`/static/`, exact paths).\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Recommended Security Track — HTTPS / TLS\n\n**Best track for a networks class:** **Transport Layer Security (TLS/HTTPS)**\n\n**Why:** It maps directly to networking concepts students are learning — handshakes, certificates, symmetric vs asymmetric encryption, certificate authorities, and port 443 vs 80.\n\n**Implementation steps (student-driven, not AI):**\n1. Generate a self-signed certificate (`openssl req -x509 ...`) or use `certstrap`/`mkcert`.\n2. Switch from `http.ListenAndServe` to `http.ListenAndServeTLS(\":8443\", \"cert.pem\", \"key.pem\", nil)`.\n3. Add HTTP→HTTPS redirect on port 8080.\n4. Explain the TLS handshake in comments/README (ClientHello, ServerHello, certificate verification, key exchange).\n5. (Advanced) Add HSTS header: `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`.\n\n**Alternative/secondary track (if TLS is too much):** **Security Headers** — `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `Referrer-Policy`. This is lighter but still valuable.\n\n---\n\n## Tips for Prompting the AI to Generate the Initial Code\n\nDon't dump one giant prompt. Use an **iterative, modular approach**:\n\n### 1. Start with context and constraints\n> \"Write a basic educational HTTP server in Go using only the standard library (`net/http`). No external frameworks. It should run on localhost:8080 and be suitable for a Computer Networks class project.\"\n\n### 2. Specify exact behavior\n> \"Support these routes:\n> - `GET /` → returns 'Hello, World' with 200\n> - `GET /hello?name=X` → returns personalized greeting, 400 if `name` missing\n> - `POST /echo` → reads body and echoes it back with `Content-Type: text/plain`\n> - Any undefined route → 404\n> - Wrong method on a route → 405\n> Set correct `Content-Type` headers and log each request to stdout.\"\n\n### 3. Ask for structure and comments\n> \"Split into `main.go` (server setup) and `handler.go` (route handlers). Add comments explaining what each HTTP status code means and how the request/response cycle works.\"\n\n### 4. Ask for a test plan\n> \"Provide `curl` commands to test each route and status code, and a short `README.md` with build/run instructions.\"\n\n### 5. Iterate — don't accept the first output blindly\n- If it uses a framework: \"Rewrite using only `net/http`.\"\n- If it lacks error handling: \"Add proper error handling for `ReadAll`, `Write`, and `ListenAndServe`.\"\n- If it's too \"production-ready\": \"Simplify — this is for learning, not production.\"\n\n### 6. Prompt for the security layer separately\n> After the baseline works: \"Now add TLS/HTTPS support using `http.ListenAndServeTLS`. Explain how to generate a self-signed certificate with OpenSSL and how the server changes.\"\n\n### 7. Have the AI explain, not just code\n> \"After generating the code, explain in plain terms: what happens when a browser sends a GET request, how Go's `ServeMux` routes it, and where the status code is set.\"\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Timeline (2–3 weeks)\n\n| Week | Focus |\n|------|-------|\n| 1 | Prompt AI → generate baseline → get it running → write `curl` tests |\n| 2 | Apply class knowledge (concurrency, timeouts, headers, status codes) |\n| 3 | Implement security track (TLS or headers) → document → present |\n\n---\n\n## One Warning for Students\n\nAI-generated code often looks correct but may miss edge cases (e.g., no read timeouts, no body size limits, improper `Content-Length`). Students should treat the AI output as a **starting point to critique and improve**, not a finished product. That critique is exactly where the class learning happens.\n\nWould you like me to draft an actual prompt template they can copy-paste into an AI, or a sample `README.md` outline for the project?"
